The SagaECO files used as inputs to the optional content audit remain in the
separate research/SagaECO checkout under that repository's GPL-3.0 license;
they are not included in or relicensed by this prototype.

The headless training-actor inspection reads the external SagaECO
Configuration/DB monster.csv and Spawns/20013003.xml records for mob
30030018, and checks them against the separately licensed terrain data
described below. These source records establish a water training sandbag at
(61,65), amount 1, range 0, delay 10, with 9999 HP. This reports facts from
external data; no SagaECO source code is copied into this prototype, and the
data remains under its separate GPL-3.0 license.

Movement wire-format facts (0x11F8/0x11F9, coordinate scale and direction
octants) were checked against that SagaECO checkout. The prototype's fallback
entrance for map 20013003 is (63,58), from portal P10000499, checked against
MapInfo.zip walkability. This is a chosen existing entrance for the prototype,
not a claim about the official new-character spawn or complete client parity.

The optional headless hunt replay reads external QuestDB_1.xml quest 10000001.
Its independently implemented single-objective counter follows numeric rule
facts checked against SagaECO MapClient.QuestMobKilled/SendQuestCount and
SkillHandler's own/shared party kill calls: mob 10000000 on map 10025000,
five kills, excluding credit shared from another party member. No SagaECO
implementation is copied. The external quest data retains its GPL-3.0 license.
The replay does not implement quest acceptance, expiry, rewards, persistence,
or live-client packets and is not evidence of playable quests.

The separate timed hunt replay additionally checks quest 10000001's Time=60
and independently models explicit time-check/kill ordering from numeric and
state-transition facts in SagaECO Event.QuestActivate, MapClient.SendQuestTime,
and MapClient.SendQuestCount. The 60-minute deadline applies on time checks;
completed quests remain completed, and failed quests cannot complete through
later kills. No SagaECO implementation is copied. No scheduler, acceptance,
rewards, persistence, or live-client quest packets are implemented.

The optional lifecycle replay independently models quest 10000001 acceptance,
explicit cancellation, deadline checks, and kills from caller-supplied initial
quest points and fame. Its numeric rules follow SagaECO Event.HandleQuest,
Event.QuestActivate, MapClient quest-count handling, and the external quest
record: acceptance spends one point, OPEN cancellation clears progress and
reduces fame by one with a zero floor, and cancellation does not refund points.
Quest MinLV=255 is treated as the source wildcard. No SagaECO implementation
is copied. The replay does not implement NPC selection or difficulty prompts,
completed-quest turn-in, rewards, persistence, combat, or live-client quest
packets. The cleanup event independently models SagaECO Event.HandleQuest
calling quest cleanup only after FAILED; it clears status, progress, and
deadline, reduces fame by one to a floor of zero, and does not refund a quest
point. No confirmation prompt is modeled. No SagaECO implementation is copied.
The external quest data retains its GPL-3.0 license.

The separate saved lifecycle command persists its independently implemented
quest state for an existing prototype character. It commits each validated
event batch with the character ownership check in one transaction.
Caller-supplied initial points and fame apply only when that character has no
saved quest state. SagaECO and EcoServerEmulator provide different
quest-point defaults and do not establish the current client's initial
values; this command accepts them explicitly. No SagaECO implementation is
copied, and the command does not implement live timers, combat, NPC dialogue,
rewards, or client packets.

The explicit initialize-novice-progress command adds an optional initial
progression row for an existing character using additive SQLite schema
version 4. The row stores only baseline values: level 1/1, EXP 0/0, stat
points 2 and skill points 3. NOVICE job, no joint and ordinary races 0-2
define this command's scope; they are not row fields or source-established
eligibility rules. Only baseline initialization is accepted, not import of
already-leveled progression. The baseline is independently encoded from
legacy numeric/default facts in SagaLogin/Network/Client/LoginClient.cs:475-478,
505-506 and SagaDB/Actor/ActorPC.cs (CEXP/JEXP are unsigned integers with
zero defaults). These sources do not verify the current client's starting
progression. Existing progression rows are preserved. No SagaECO code is
copied; this headless initializer does not implement automatic character
initialization, leveling, live packets, inventory, or quest turn-in.

The explicit initialize-novice-base-stats command stores the six configured
ordinary-race starting attributes in optional save fields. Its independent
numeric table follows SagaECO Configuration/Config/SagaLogin.xml StartStatus
records (Emil: STR/DEX/INT/VIT/AGI/MAG = 8/3/3/10/4/3; Titania =
6/3/6/4/2/10; Dominion = 10/5/1/1/5/8); SagaLogin/Network/Client/LoginClient.cs
assigns these configured fields to the new character. The DEM record is
validated as part of the source file but remains outside this command's
ordinary-race scope. These are numeric source facts only: no SagaECO
implementation or configuration file is copied into the prototype, and the
external source retains its separate GPL-3.0 license. This command does not
initialize characters automatically or add starting inventory.

The explicit initialize-starter-inventory command records the five supplied
StartItem entries for each ordinary race and gender in SagaLogin.xml. Its item
IDs, counts and container names are independently read from those records and
validated against the external SagaECO Configuration/DB/item.csv catalog; the
two DEM groups are checked but excluded from the ordinary-character snapshot.
The snapshot records the five configured item IDs and the persistence fields
needed to represent those rows. UPPER_BODY, LOWER_BODY and SHOES use the three
equipped containers; both BODY rows remain in BODY inventory, including the
sword. Internal slot keys identify Rust snapshot rows and are not imported
legacy DBIDs. An absent snapshot is initialized atomically; partial or
conflicting rows are refused.
No SagaECO implementation or configuration data is copied into the prototype,
and the external sources retain their separate GPL-3.0 license. This additive
schema version 6 command requires an already initialized novice progress row
and does not run automatically, grant quest rewards, implement generic item
state, update inventory, equip items, calculate status, or send live packets.

The starter catalog validator also checks zero HP/MP/SP and six primary-stat
bonuses on the thirteen equipped wearable IDs, using the column mappings in
SagaDB/Item/ItemFactory.cs:105-116 and equipment summation facts in
SagaMap/PC/StatusFactory.cs:1125-1133. BODY items are excluded from these bonus
checks. Catalog checks do not establish absent item-instance or other status
modifiers; no status calculation or SagaECO implementation is copied here.

The separate apply-novice-exp command uses caller-supplied, already-scaled
base and first-job EXP deltas. Without explicit persisted status context,
totals remain bounded to 0..55. Schema v7 can store an explicit unmodified
ordinary NOVICE declaration and calculated/current HP/MP/SP. With initialized
race stats, exact starter inventory and map 20013003/flags 0, cumulative EXP
may reach 155; strict EXP >55 advances each track independently to level 2.
The external exp.xml supplies thresholds 0/55/155. ExperienceManager.cs:
956-1040 establishes +3 base stat points, +1 first-job skill point, strict
comparison and level-up refill. StatusFactory.cs:423-473,480-485,570-575,
660-665,749-777 supplies resource arithmetic and novice factors. MapInfo.cs
and MapInfoFactory.LoadFlags establish default zero flags when the target
map has no entry in the external MapFlags.xml. The command validates those
external inputs and starter catalog fields before opening a save.
These numeric/source facts inform independent Rust arithmetic; no SagaECO
implementation or external table is embedded. The declaration establishes
the limited headless state explicitly, not by inferring missing old columns.
This is not automatic combat EXP, quest reward delivery or client proof.

The initialize-gold command saves explicitly supplied carried gold once in an
optional schema v8 row. ActorPC.cs:1376-1394 establishes the accepted property
range 0..99,999,999; AccessActorDb.cs:307 hydrates that property. Rust refuses
out-of-range input rather than clamping it. Migration leaves gold unknown.
This independent persistence prerequisite grants no rewards. Source review of
Event.cs:307-324 and ExperienceManager.cs:351-429 confirms that complete quest
turn-in also requires EXP, CP, EP, fame, ring and item effects. The supplied
SagaMap.xml:65-67 sets QuestRate=100 and QuestGoldRate=600; the listed 80-gold
reward therefore implies 480 at that configuration, not an automatic grant.

The initialize-reward-context command explicitly stores caller-known EP and CP
and a caller declaration of no ring in optional schema v9. Migration leaves
these values unknown. EP is bounded to 0..30 for this ringless ordinary novice
context, based on StatusFactory.cs:389-415; CP is bounded to 0..99,999,999 by
ActorPC.cs:1400-1415. Matching repeat initialization preserves the values and
conflicts refuse. The exact --declare-no-ring flag is required; missing data
never establishes ring absence. Initialize novice progression, base stats and
starter inventory, then use apply-novice-exp ... 0 0 --declare-unmodified-status
to declare the required ordinary novice status before this command. The status
initializer relies on those base stats and the exact starter snapshot. Source
EP leveling behavior preserves current EP subject to MaxEP; this context uses
the source-supported ringless MaxEP30. This command does not deliver rewards,
and COMPLETED quests remain occupied. Older binaries refuse schema v9 saves.

The initialize-reward-inventory command adds optional schema v10 context and
separate reward-instance storage while preserving the five starter rows.
Its explicit declaration establishes a complete initial inventory; migration
does not infer completeness or grant items. Inventory.cs:55,489-631 uses a
shared uint slot sequence across BODY/bags/equipment. Rust assigns local keys
after its five starter keys, not imported legacy IDs. ItemFactory.cs:194-202,
Item.cs:23-30,60-70,235-250,988-992 and Event.cs:902-942 establish new-instance
defaults: count1, catalog durability, identified, PictID0, unmodified and
non-rental with no cards. Rental time is irrelevant for this non-rental scope.
Quest/config/catalog fields are validated externally before opening a save.
No table text or legacy implementation is embedded. The virtual quest-update
hook depends on the handling NPC Event instance (Event.cs:307-324,408-414);
missing NPC context cannot establish a no-op. No turn-in or rewards are granted
by this prerequisite; full gameplay and English coverage remain unverified.

The explicit turn-in-completed-quest command accepts only the existing
COMPLETED lifecycle row for quest 10000001 and requires the caller's exact
--declare-npc-event-11000435 declaration. Source review confirms Event 11000435
inherits an empty OnQuestUpdate; the flag declares the scoped headless
HandleQuest invocation context on that Event instance. It does not mean rewards
were already applied, or run OnEvent or HandleQuest, and does not establish NPC
placement, live routing, dialogue, or a playable quest.
The completed branch runs before new-acceptance group filtering. The command
validates the external quest/reward definitions, SagaMap rates, EXP table,
starter/reward catalog and map flags before opening the existing save, then
atomically applies the bounded source-derived turn-in to a character with
explicitly initialized progression/status, stats, complete inventory, gold,
EP/CP and no-ring context. Copy the save first.
For this quest and supplied map rates, it grants base/job EXP 55/55, gold 480,
CP 0, EP +1 up to 30, fame +1, and item60060000 in the next shared BODY slot
with the supported default instance fields, then clears COMPLETED. EXP totals
cannot exceed 155 and fame cannot exceed 2,147,483,647; integer overflow,
unsupported state and missing context refuse the transaction. A repeated
turn-in has no COMPLETED row and refuses without grants. Generic replay cleanup
remains FAILED-only. These are source facts and bounded offline behavior, not
evidence of live NPC dispatch or complete client compatibility.

The separately licensed extracted start-map data and its original GPL-3.0
license/provenance are in data/COPYING and data/NOTICE.txt. The Rust executable
loads this external file; it does not embed the dataset.

The experimental standalone text-archive research reader uses format facts
checked against https://github.com/coremaze/AECO-Archive. Its limited decoder
was independently implemented; no source from that unlicensed repository is
bundled here. This reader is not part of the server executable.

The dictionary insertion depth limit of 100 bytes was checked against
compression/src/compression.cpp:27-40 at AECO-Archive commit
255d84cbf6cba475dd19a71e6b1e3c8d4407a8fe. Independent Rust code limits the
combined previous/current phrase path accordingly. Synthetic code sequences
and read-only supplied archive checks verify this bounded extension; no
unlicensed implementation or client table payload is bundled.

Dictionary recycling rules were checked against the same pinned source,
compression/src/compression.cpp:36-117 and 295-345, with array layout from
compression/include/compression.hpp. The independent Rust implementation
orders all phrase codes so descendants precede ancestors, touches expanded
phrase nodes from leaf to root, places new children before phrase parents
(or at the tail for literal parents), and reuses the head after removing its
old child edge. If the head is the parent being extended, that update stops.
The source contains apparently unreachable branches; their intent was not
guessed. Synthetic regression tests and read-only supplied archive checks
cover this extension. Selecting one archive ordinal does not certify other
payloads, text encoding, translations, or runtime client compatibility.

The standalone map-text byte audit reads the supplied Japanese table archive
in memory and compares numeric first-field candidates with entry basenames
from the external SagaECO MapInfo.zip. The source reader uses those basenames
as effective map IDs (SagaDB/Map/MapInfoFactory.cs:228). No table text, map
payload, translation, or SagaECO implementation is copied into the tool.
The external legacy metadata retains its separate GPL-3.0 license. The audit
does not establish the Japanese tables' encoding/schema, English label
coverage, or current-client compatibility; its report contains only static
labels and aggregate counts.

The map-text row filter was independently implemented from bounded static
inspection of the supplied Japanese executable: row initializer VA 0x9B97B0
skips CR/LF-only records and records beginning with byte 0x23 (check at
0x9B985E), without trimming spaces first. Only this record-selection rule
is adopted; the audit keeps its stricter framing and unsupported-quote guards.
No executable, disassembly, decoded client text or proprietary implementation
is bundled. The standalone byte audit validates supplied table bytes as CP932
using Windows MultiByteToWideChar with MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS and an explicit
input length; it emits no converted text and does not establish runtime
encoding or table meaning. Table-specific conversion uses a code-page global
initialized to 932. The supplied `strtable.csv` table entry includes the
literal record CLIENT_DATA_CODE_PAGE,"932"; the audit accepts that guarded
form. Whether the client loads this file and uses that value at runtime is
unverified. See the Windows API documentation:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/stringapiset/nf-stringapiset-multibytetowidechar.

The current single-player map view uses a runtime player actor key of 16,
separate from the durable character primary key. This numeric convention was
checked against research/SagaECO/SagaMap/Map.cs at the GPL-3.0 revision above
and PlutoProject's map actor allocation at karorogunso/SagaECO commit
43d90a1bfd1f7ac1ee2172e7c8377953a1b67099. Their implementations were not copied
or translated into Rust. The change is independently implemented from numeric
identity facts and target-client evidence: the old durable key was already
present in the client's actor tree before map entry, and the native player
initializer rejects duplicate tree keys. See research/CSHARP_REFERENCE_PORT.md
and research/rea/README.md for source and current-build verification limits.
The fixed runtime key is scoped to one player per map view; it is not a shared
world allocator or a co-op implementation. Existing save identities are retained.
